Ways the change could fail a criterion (what each rating is measured against):

  smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true
    [other] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/descriptor-can-return-non-minimal-edit
      The descriptor stage can return an edit that is not the smallest one that makes the defect true, because there is no mechanical check that the model chose the minimal span or that a shorter valid edit was rejected.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
    [missing_case] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/descriptor-declines-when-edit-exists
      A defect that does have a valid single-span edit can still be classified as not_mutable if the descriptor call returns an empty region label or the runner rejects the descriptor too aggressively, so the review may skip building an edit even though one exists.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0
    [scope_too_narrow] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/region-filter-drops-valid-edit-sites
      The descriptor only asks about regions whose file path is present in the preloaded sources, so a defect whose smallest edit is in a named region from a file that was not loaded or was omitted from sources will never get a chance to produce the required edit.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
    [other] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/mutation-copy-applies-edit-to-wrong-file
      The injected edit could be applied to the wrong file if descriptor.path does not match the intended region or if the copy step targets the wrong path, so the throwaway copy would not contain the smallest edit for the named defect.
      src/acceptance/mutation/injection.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
    [missing_case] smallest-edit-that-makes-defect-true/validity-rejects-prose-defects-as-unmutable
      The validity check only parses .py, .pyi, and .json files, so a defect in prose or another unparsed format can be rejected as not_mutable even when a smallest contiguous edit exists in that text.
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0

  run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy
    [scope_too_narrow] run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy/execution-tier-disabled-by-default
      The new execution tier is opt-in only, so the candidate tests are not run against the altered copy unless the caller passes --execute and the pipeline receives enabled execution settings.
      src/acceptance/cli.py#3
      src/acceptance/cli.py#4
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#3
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#4
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
    [missing_case] run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy/baseline-halt-skips-mutation-run
      If the control run finds any candidate test already failing at head and --allow-failing-tests is not set, the code raises ReviewHalted and never runs the candidate tests against the altered copy.
      src/acceptance/mutation/baseline.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/cli.py#4
    [missing_case] run-candidate-tests-on-altered-copy/not-attempted-mutants-never-run-tests
      When a defect is judged not_mutable or not_attempted, the runner returns before copying the project and before calling run_tests, so those defects never get candidate tests executed against an altered copy.
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0

  test-fails-discriminate-defect
    [other] test-fails-discriminate-defect/failing-tests-not-marked-as-discriminating
      When a candidate test fails against the altered copy, the execution verdict is recorded with `kills=True` and `tier=DEFECT_KILLED`, but the code never explicitly marks the corresponding review finding or pair verdict as 'discriminates for that defect'; if the downstream review/reporting path only treats `kills` as a generic failure signal, the review could omit the discrimination claim the criterion asks for.
      src/acceptance/mutation/verdicts.py#0
      src/acceptance/review_state.py#1
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#5
      src/acceptance/report.py#0
    [not_wired] test-fails-discriminate-defect/executed-verdicts-may-not-reach-discrimination-record
      The execution tier builds `executed_verdicts`, but the review only records discrimination if those verdicts are actually threaded into the same structures the rest of the pipeline uses; if any caller path bypasses `_run_execution_tier` or drops `executed_verdicts` before persistence, a failing test would not be shown as discriminating for the defect.
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#2
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#4
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#5
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#7
      src/acceptance/review_state.py#1
    [scope_too_narrow] test-fails-discriminate-defect/survival-path-only-records-failures
      `verdicts_from` emits verdicts only for settled attempts, so if a mutant is classified as `NOT_ATTEMPTED` because some tests did not complete, any failing candidate test from that run is not recorded as discriminating for the defect even though the criterion is about a test that fails against the altered copy.
      src/acceptance/mutation/verdicts.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/attempt.py#0

